00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:10.860
There are some similarities between the sequence-to-sequence machine translation model and the language models that you have worked with in the first week of this course.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:15.020
But there are some significant differences as well. Let's take a look.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:21.660
So you can think of machine translation as building a conditional language model. Here's what I mean.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:27.100
In language modeling, this was the network we had built in the first week.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:37.900
And this model allows you to estimate the probability of a sentence. That's what a language model does.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:42.060
And you can also use this to generate novel sentences.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:53.100
And sometimes we were writing x1 and x2 here, where in this example, x2 would be equal to y1 or equal to y1 is just a feedback.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:59.580
But x1, x2 and so on weren't important. So just to clean this up for this slide, I'm going to just cross these on.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:05.740
Where x1 could be the vector of all zeros and x2, x3 are just the previous output you were generating.

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:12.220
So that was the language model. The machine translation model looks as follows.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:21.420
I'm going to use a couple different colors, green and purple, to denote respectively the encoder network in green and the decoder network in purple.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:33.260
And you notice that the decoder network looks pretty much identical to the language model that we had up there.

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:43.580
So what the machine translation model is, is very similar to the language model, except that instead of always starting it off with the vector of all zeros,

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:51.900
it instead has an encoder network that figures out some representation for the input sentence, and it takes that input sentence

00:01:51.900 --> 00:02:00.940
and starts off the decoder network with representation of the input sentence rather than with the representation of all zeros.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:06.780
So that's why I call this a conditional language model.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:16.940
And instead of modeling the probability of any sentence, it is now modeling the probability of, say, the output English translation,

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:28.540
conditions on some input French sentence. So in other words, you're trying to estimate the probability of a English translation,

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:33.020
like what's the chance that the translation is Jane is visiting Africa in September,

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:41.020
but conditions on the input French sentence, like Jean visit the freak on September.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:48.460
So this is really the probability of an English sentence conditions on an input French sentence,

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:54.860
which is why it is a conditional language model. Now, if you want to apply this model to actually

00:02:54.860 --> 00:03:01.420
translate a sentence from French into English, given this input French sentence,

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:08.300
the model might tell you what is the probability of different corresponding English translations.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:13.900
So this is X is the French sentence, Jean visit the freak on September.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:21.420
And this now tells you what is the probability of different English translations of that French input.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:27.740
And what you do not want is to sample outputs at random.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:36.220
If you sample words from this distribution, p of y given x, maybe one time you get a pretty good translation.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:40.780
Jane is visiting Africa in September, but maybe another time you get a different translation.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:46.700
Jane's going to be visiting Africa in September, which sounds a little awkward, but it's not a terrible translation,

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:52.700
just not the best one. And sometimes just by chance you get study others in September,

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:57.900
Jane visits Africa. And maybe just by chance, sometimes you sample a really bad translation,

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:04.540
African friend, welcome Jane in September. So when you're using this model for machine translation,

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:10.380
you're not trying to sample at random from this distribution. Instead, what you would like

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:16.220
is to find a English sentence y that maximizes that conditional probability.

00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:22.940
So in developing a machine translation system, one of the things you need to do is come up

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:31.660
with an algorithm that can actually find the value of y that maximizes this term over here.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:35.900
The most common algorithm for doing this, called the beam search, is something you see

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:40.700
in the next video. But before moving on to the striped beam search, you might wonder,

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:46.700
why not just use greedy search? So what is greedy search? Well greedy search is an algorithm from

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:52.060
computer science which says to generate the first word, just pick whatever is the most likely first

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:58.860
word according to your conditional language model, according to your machine translation model.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:03.420
And then after having picked the first word, you then pick whatever is the second word that

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:08.700
seems most likely, and then pick the third word that seems most likely. This algorithm is called

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:18.300
greedy search. And what you would really like is to pick the entire sequence of words, y1, y2,

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:26.780
up to y, ty, with hats there, that maximizes the joint probability of that whole thing.

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:31.980
And it turns out that the greedy approach, where you just pick the best first word,

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:36.700
and then after having picked the best first word, try to pick the best second word, and then

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:42.460
after that try to pick the best third word, that approach doesn't really work. To illustrate that,

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:48.140
let's consider following two translations. The first one is a better translation. So hopefully,

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:55.660
in our machine translation model, it will say that p of y given x is higher for the first

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:59.980
sentence. This is a better, more succinct translation of the French input. And the

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:05.500
second one is not a bad translation. It's just more verbal says more unnecessary words.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:12.060
But if the algorithm has picked Jane is as the first two words, because going is a more

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:21.820
common English word, probably the chance of Jane is going. Given the French input,

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:30.220
this might actually be higher than the chance of Jane is visiting, given the input sentence,

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:36.860
input French sentence. And so it's quite possible that if you just pick the third word, based on

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:42.620
whatever maximizes the probability of just the first three words, you end up choosing option

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:51.820
number two. But this ultimately ends up resulting in a less optimal sentence, in a less good sentence

00:06:51.820 --> 00:07:01.230
as measured by this model for p of y given x. I know this was maybe a slightly hand-wavy argument,

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:08.110
but this is an example of a broader phenomenon where if you want to find the sequence of words

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:13.630
y1, y2, all the way up to the final word that together maximize the probability,

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:21.470
it's not always optimal to just pick one word at a time. And of course, the total number of combinations

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:28.830
of words in a English sentence is exponentially large. So if you have just 10,000 words in a

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:37.390
dictionary, and if you're contemplating translations are up to 10 words long, then there are 10,000

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:45.150
to the 10 possible sentences that are 10 words long, picking words from the vocabulary size of,

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:51.070
from the dictionary size of 10,000 words. So this is just a huge space of possible sentences,

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:56.590
and it's impossible to enumerate them all, which is why the most common thing to do is to use an

00:07:56.590 --> 00:08:03.150
approximate search algorithm. And what an approximate search algorithm does is it will

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:09.550
try, it won't always succeed, but it will try to pick the sentence y that maximizes

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:15.790
that conditional probability. And even though it's not guaranteed to find the value of y that

00:08:15.790 --> 00:08:21.310
maximizes this, it usually does a good enough job. So to summarize, in this video, you saw how

00:08:21.310 --> 00:08:27.950
machine translation can be posed as a conditional language modeling problem. But one major difference

00:08:27.950 --> 00:08:32.910
between this and the earlier language modeling problems is rather than wanting to generate a

00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:38.430
sentence at random, you maybe want to try to find the most likely English sentence, the most likely

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:45.310
English translation. But the set of all English sentences of a certain length is too large to

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:52.110
exhaustively enumerate. So we'll have to resort to a search algorithm. So with that, let's go on

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:56.030
to the next video where you learn about the beam search algorithm.
